  # Bootstrapping a Cisco Catalyst 8000V on GCP — SSH key auth that works on first boot

Standalone recipe. No dependency on any framework — plain Terraform you can
paste into a greenfield project.

Verified end to end on `cisco-c8k-17-16-01a-byol`: first boot, pubkey-only,
`BatchMode=yes` (so a silent password fallback can't fake a pass).

---

## The one thing to understand first

The c8000v has **no Google guest agent**. Cisco's own day-0 agent reads
instance metadata, and it has **two mutually exclusive paths**:

| Metadata you set | Agent path | What you get |
|---|---|---|
| `ssh-keys` only | **auto** | Agent writes its own minimal config: **Gi1 up on DHCP, SSH enabled**, and binds every `user:key` entry you supplied |
| **any** `startup-script` | **customer-config** | Agent applies *your* config via CVAC — and **silently stops binding `ssh-keys`** |

⚠ **This is the trap.** Adding a startup script — even a trivial one — turns
off the `ssh-keys` mechanism with no error anywhere. The box boots healthy,
the metadata is present and correct, and your key is simply never installed.

So pick a recipe based on whether you need boot-time config, and never mix.

---

## Recipe A — metadata only (simplest; use this if you can)

Best for a **single-NIC** box, or any box you'll configure over SSH after it
boots.

```hcl
resource "google_compute_instance" "c8k" {
  name         = "c8k-1"
  machine_type = "n2-standard-4"
  zone         = "us-east4-a"

  boot_disk {
    initialize_params {
      image = "projects/cisco-public/global/images/cisco-c8k-17-16-01a-byol"
    }
  }

  network_interface {
    subnetwork = google_compute_subnetwork.lab.id
  }

  # ⚠ THE ONLY METADATA KEY. Adding startup-script here breaks key binding.
  # Standard GCE format: newline-separated user:key entries.
  metadata = {
    ssh-keys = "edadmin:${trimspace(file("~/.ssh/c8k_ed25519.pub"))}"
  }
}

# IAP ingress — 35.235.240.0/20 is Google's IAP forwarding range.
resource "google_compute_firewall" "iap" {
  name          = "fw-c8k-iap"
  network       = google_compute_network.lab.id
  source_ranges = ["35.235.240.0/20"]
  allow {
    protocol = "tcp"
    ports    = ["22"]
  }
}
```

Connect — **stock gcloud, no flags**:

```bash
gcloud compute ssh edadmin@c8k-1 --zone=us-east4-a --tunnel-through-iap \
  --ssh-key-file=~/.ssh/c8k_ed25519
```

---

## Recipe B — startup-script (when you need boot-time config)

The moment you need *any* day-0 config — bringing up data NICs, for example —
you're on the customer-config path, so the key bindings must move **inside**
the config.

```hcl
locals {
  # ⚠ MD5 FINGERPRINT, NOT THE KEY. Compute it offline:
  #     ssh-keygen -l -E md5 -f ~/.ssh/c8k_ed25519.pub
  # Take the hex after "MD5:" and STRIP THE COLONS.
  ed_md5 = "2497e2d8d319abf009251061df66f9d1"

  day0 = <<-IOS
    Section: IOS configuration
    hostname c8k-1
    interface GigabitEthernet1
     ip address dhcp
     no shutdown
    interface GigabitEthernet2
     ip address 10.10.0.11 255.255.255.0
     no shutdown
    username edadmin privilege 15
    ip ssh pubkey-chain
    username edadmin
    key-hash ssh-ed25519 ${local.ed_md5}
    exit
    exit
  IOS
}

resource "google_compute_instance" "c8k" {
  # ... as above ...

  # ⚠ NO ssh-keys KEY HERE — it would be ignored on this path. The binding
  # is the key-hash line inside day0.
  metadata = {
    startup-script = local.day0
  }
}
```

Three things that will bite you in Recipe B:

- **`Section: IOS configuration` is a required envelope.** Without that exact
  first line the agent doesn't recognise the payload as bootstrap config and
  silently ignores it.
- **`key-hash`, never `key-string`.** Pasting the full public key into
  `pubkey-chain` fails under CVAC. Only the MD5-fingerprint one-liner works.
- **It is `startup-script`, not `user-data`.** Same metadata transport, but
  nothing is *executed* — there's no shell. Cisco's agent just reads the
  string. (`user-data` is the cloud-init convention and is wrong here.)

---

## Use ED25519, not RSA

RSA works, but IOS-XE only signs RSA with SHA-1 (`ssh-rsa`), which modern
OpenSSH disables by default. You'll see `no mutual signature algorithm` and
need:

```bash
gcloud compute ssh admin@c8k-1 ... -- \
  -o PubkeyAcceptedAlgorithms=+ssh-rsa -o HostKeyAlgorithms=+ssh-rsa
```

ED25519 is offered natively by the server and needs no flags. Generate with:

```bash
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -f ~/.ssh/c8k_ed25519 -N ''
ssh-keygen -l -E md5 -f ~/.ssh/c8k_ed25519.pub   # for Recipe B's key-hash
```

---

## ⚠ Multi-NIC boxes: what the auto path does NOT do

Recipe A's auto path brings up **Gi1 only**. On a multi-NIC c8000v the data
interfaces boot **administratively down**, so:

- The box is **reachable** (Gi1 + IAP) — you can log in and raise them.
- But it forwards nothing until you do.

That's the real choice: Recipe A gives you a reachable box you finish by
hand; Recipe B gives you a fully configured box at the cost of moving key
management into the config. There is no option that does both automatically.

---

## Verifying it actually worked

Test in strict terms — `BatchMode=yes` prevents a password prompt from
masking a failed key:

```bash
gcloud compute ssh edadmin@c8k-1 --zone=us-east4-a --tunnel-through-iap \
  --ssh-key-file=~/.ssh/c8k_ed25519 -- -o BatchMode=yes 'show ip int brief'
```

If it fails, read the serial console before changing anything — it tells you
which agent path ran:

```bash
gcloud compute instances get-serial-port-output c8k-1 --zone=us-east4-a
```

- `Customer bootstrap cfg provided in custom-data format!` → customer-config
  path (Recipe B). `ssh-keys` is being ignored.
- `No supported customer bootstrap...` → auto path (Recipe A). Keys bound.

---

## Why this was hard to diagnose

Five problems stacked, each masking the next:

1. Multi-NIC box with no day-0 → **every interface admin-down**. Keys were
   bound correctly; there was no L3 path to authenticate over. The mechanism
   looked broken and was innocent.
2. Once reachable, RSA/SHA-1 rejection looked like "key rejected" but was
   entirely client-side.
3. One boot with a startup-script happened to take the **auto** path anyway
   (serial: "No supported customer bootstrap"), so both mechanisms appeared
   to work together. They don't — that was a race.
4. Later boots took the customer path, key binding stopped, and the failure
   looked intermittent.
5. `key-string` in `pubkey-chain` fails under CVAC where it works
   interactively.

If you're debugging this: **check the serial console for the agent path
first.** It converts a guessing game into a two-case decision.


